@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { supported host, parent adapter, host }

component AgentConfig {
  goal {
    Load and validate the spike's agent configuration so that every other component
    receives an agent definition whose host, model and thinking level come from one
    model tier and whose skills are absolute folders.
  }

  state {
    The configuration is one *agent file*, .agents/subzero.yaml, with a
    *skills map* from skill name to *skill folder*, an *endpoints map*, a
    *models map* and an *agents map* of named agents, each an *agent entry*.

    The models map holds a *tiers map* that gives each *model tier* by name a host,
    a
    *model* and a *thinking level*. The model tier named default is the
    *default tier*. The host, the model and the thinking level live only in model
    tiers and in tier overrides, and never directly in an agent entry.

    An agent entry has a *description*, the names of its skills, a *tool list* and
    a
    *timeout*, and may name a model tier as its own tier and hold a *tier override*
    for any model tier. The entries named general and fallback have no description.
    A tier override replaces, for that agent only, any of the host, the model and
    the thinking level of the model tier it names, and leaves the rest of that
    model tier as it is.

    An *agent definition* is one agent entry resolved at one model tier: the tier
    that the request names, or else the entry's own tier, or else the default tier.
    It has the description, the skills, the tool list and the timeout of the entry,
    and the host, the model and the thinking level of that model tier after the
    entry's tier override for it. It is named after the entry, and named
    <entry>:<tier> when the request names a model tier.

    The endpoints map holds an *endpoints entry* for a *model endpoint*. A
    model endpoint is the part of a model before its first slash, and each
    agent definition belongs to the model endpoint of its model. The entry holds
    the *parallel limit* of that model endpoint, a positive whole number that is
    the most child runs that one process runs at the same time on it. A
    model endpoint with no entry has no parallel limit, and every agent definition
    on the same model endpoint shares its parallel limit.

    The timeout is a required number of seconds. It limits the wall-clock time of a
    child run from its spawn to its settlement, including skill verification, and
    it does not run while the child run is Pending.

    The agents map may hold an entry named general, the *general agent*, which
    takes work that names no agent or names general, and whose tool list and
    timeout tiered work from a parent uses.

    The agents map may also hold an entry named fallback, the *fallback agent*,
    which takes the work of a name other than general that the agents map does not
    hold. Its tool list can be limited to tools that only read, and a front end's
    init writes it that way. Neither the general agent nor the fallback agent is
    chosen by its description or offered to a parent for routing. The default tier,
    the general agent and the fallback agent that a front end's init adds are the
    *starter entries*.

    A *routable tier* is a model tier whose host, after the tier override of the
    general agent, is a supported host, or any model tier when the caller gives no
    supported hosts, and only while the agents map holds a general agent, because
    tiered work runs on the general agent. The default tier is a routable tier on
    the same terms as any other, because the general agent runs on it when no tier
    is named, and a front end that routes by tier word leaves it out because it
    has none.

    The agent file may also hold *routing settings*, which are a *hook switch* that
    is on unless it is set to false, and an optional *generic agent list* of agent
    type names.
  }

  logic {
    On load, read the agent file, validate every model tier, every agent entry, the
    general agent and the fallback agent included, every tier override and the
    routing settings against the schema, and resolve each skill folder relative to
    the directory of the agent file into an absolute path. The flow returns the
    agent entries, the model tiers and the routable tier names to its caller, or
    stops with a *load error* that names the offending entry, or says that the
    agent file is missing, unreadable or malformed. One invalid model tier or agent
    entry fails the whole load.

    On a request for an agent at a model tier, resolve the agent entry at the tier
    the request names, or else at the entry's own tier, or else at the
    default tier, apply the entry's tier override for that tier, and return the
    agent definition, which ends the flow. When the tier is not configured, or its
    host after the override is not a supported host, return the tier error and end
    the flow.
  }

  constraints {
    A model tier must be rejected with a load error that names it when it has no
    host, no model or no thinking level. A tier override must be rejected with a
    load error that names its agent and its model tier when it names a model tier
    that is not configured or holds anything other than a host, a model or a
    thinking level. An agent entry must be rejected, naming it, when its own tier
    is not a configured model tier, or when it holds a host, a model or a
    thinking level directly.

    The agent file must be rejected with a load error that names the default tier
    when its agents map holds an agent entry and its models map holds no
    default tier.

    AgentConfig takes the supported hosts from its caller, which gets them from
    Kernel, and AgentConfig names no host itself. When its caller gives no
    supported hosts, AgentConfig checks no host. A model tier whose host is not a
    supported host still loads and is not a routable tier.

    The general agent and the fallback agent must each be rejected with a
    load error that starts with its name when it holds a description, because
    neither is chosen by its description.

    An endpoints entry must be rejected with a load error that names its
    model endpoint when its parallel limit is not a positive whole number, or when
    no model of a model tier or of a tier override belongs to its model endpoint.

    An agent entry must be rejected, naming it, when it has no timeout or its
    timeout is not a positive number of seconds.

    An agent entry that names a skill missing from the skills map must be rejected
    with the agent and the skill both named.

    The fallback agent must keep the tool list that the agent file gives it, at
    every model tier, and its tool list must not change the tool list of the
    general agent. AgentConfig names no tool, because tool names belong to the
    host.

    A skill folder that does not exist on disk must still load. The missing folder
    is reported only when a child is launched.

    A request that resolves an agent at a model tier that is not configured, or
    whose host after the tier override is not a supported host, must return a
    *tier error* that names the tier it resolved to, lists every configured
    model tier and, when the host is the cause, names that host and every
    supported host, and it must not fail the load, whether the host belongs to a
    model tier that still loads or to a tier override. The routable tiers are only
    a
    listing of the model tiers that the general agent can run, for the tool
    description and the hook, and whether any agent runs at a model tier is decided
    by the host of its resolved agent definition.

    AgentConfig must read the agent file again on each call that asks for an
    agent definition, a model tier or the routing settings, so that an edit to it
    is picked up without a restart.

    AgentConfig must read only the one agent file, and it writes that file only to
    add the starter entries that its interface describes. It must not merge layers,
    write subzero.lock or ask for repo trust in the spike, because those belong to
    the agent definition format of a later version.

    When it adds the starter entries, AgentConfig must leave every existing entry
    and line of the agent file unchanged, add only the default tier, the
    general agent or the fallback agent when the agent file does not hold it yet,
    write nothing when the file exists and does not load for a reason other than a
    missing default tier, add the default tier to a file that holds agent entries
    and no default tier, and write entries that load.

    AgentConfig resolves the *project root* from *SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR* first, then
    from the roots that the parent adapter reports, and for the command line from
    its working directory. It must never use the working directory of the server
    process. It keeps no project root between calls, because DelegationService asks
    once for each process and hands the answer to the other components. It finds
    the agent file under the project root that its caller hands over.

    An empty SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR counts as unset. When the parent adapter reports
    several roots, the first one reported wins. The project root must be an
    absolute path with its symbolic links resolved. When no source gives a
    project root, AgentConfig must return an *unresolved root error* that names
    SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR as the setting to fix, and must resolve again on the next
    call.
  }

  cases {
    A file with the default tier set to the host pi, the model r9700/Qwen3.8 and
    the thinking level medium, and one agent entry with no tier of its own, loads,
    and that agent definition has that host, model and thinking level and absolute
    skill folder entries.

    With the model tiers default, small, medium and large, the general agent
    resolves to four agent definitions: general on the default tier, and
    general:small, general:medium and general:large on the tier each names.

    A reviewer entry whose own tier is medium resolves to the medium model tier
    when the request names no tier, and to the large model tier, named
    reviewer:large, when the request names large. A tier override of the reviewer
    for large that sets only the model keeps the host and the thinking level of
    large.

    An agent entry that holds a model directly gives a load error that names it,
    and so does a tier override that names a model tier that is not configured.

    An agents map with entries and no default tier gives a load error that names
    the default tier. A file with the model tiers default and medium and an
    agents map that holds no general agent loads and has no routable tier, and the
    same file with a general agent has the routable tiers default and medium.

    A model tier large with the host claude, when only pi is a supported host,
    loads, is not a routable tier, and a request for it gives the tier error that
    names claude and pi. When the caller gives no supported hosts, large is a
    routable tier.

    A name that is not configured gives an unknown agent error that lists every
    configured agent entry. AgentConfig only reports the error, and
    DelegationService decides whether the fallback agent runs instead.

    Adding the starter entries to an agent file that holds general and reviewer
    entries and no default tier adds the default tier and the fallback agent and
    leaves the general and reviewer entries as they were. Adding the
    starter entries to an agent file that holds a default tier and a general agent
    and no fallback agent adds only the fallback agent, and every
    existing line stays as it was. Adding them where no agent file exists creates
    the file with all three.

    With no SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR and no root reported by the parent adapter, the
    project root is not resolved and the caller gets the unresolved root error.

    Two agent definitions on the model endpoint r9700, whose entry has a
    parallel limit of 1, are both given that parallel limit of 1, and an
    agent definition on a model endpoint with no entry is given none. An entry for
    r9770 that no model of a model tier or tier override belongs to gives a
    load error that names r9770.

    An agent file with one invalid agent entry gives a load error that names it and
    returns no agent definition, not even a valid one.

    A fallback agent that holds a description gives a load error that names it.
    With a general agent whose tool list holds read, write and bash and a
    fallback agent whose tool list holds only read, the fallback agent holds only
    read at every model tier, and general:medium keeps read, write and bash. A file
    with a general agent and no fallback agent loads, and its fallback agent is
    absent.

    The routing settings with the hook switch set to false and a custom
    generic agent list are read back as given.
  }

  interface {
    Resolve an agent entry of the agents map by name at a requested model tier, at
    its own tier or at the default tier, and provide the agent definition with its
    host, its model, its thinking level, its tool list, its timeout, its
    model endpoint with the parallel limit of that model endpoint or none, and
    every skill folder resolved to an absolute folder. The general agent and the
    fallback agent resolve the same way when the agents map holds them.

    Report which starter entries it added and which the agent file already held,
    and add the starter entries to the agent file under a project root that its
    caller hands over: the default tier from a host, a model and a thinking level,
    and the general agent and the fallback agent from the two tool lists and a
    timeout, each only when the agent file does not hold it yet, with no skills,
    creating the agent file, its models map and its agents map when missing. Refuse
    with the load error when the file exists and does not load for a reason other
    than a missing default tier, which it adds, and refuse with an error that names
    both hosts, writing nothing, when the file already holds a
    default tier whose host differs from the host that the caller gives.

    List the routable tier names, the default tier among them, with the host and
    the model each one runs for the general agent, apart from the names of every
    configured model tier.

    Provide the routing settings with the hook switch and the generic agent list as
    the agent file gives them.

    List the names of every agent entry of the agents map so that a caller can
    report them in an error.

    Return an *unknown agent error* that names the unknown name and every
    configured agent entry when a name is not configured, so that a front end can
    show it. When no agent is named, the error says that no agent was named and
    still lists every configured agent entry.

    Return the tier error that names the model tier an agent resolved to, every
    configured model tier and, when the host is the cause, that host and every
    supported host, when the agent cannot run at that model tier.

    Return a load error that names the invalid model tier or agent entry, or says
    that the agent file is missing, unreadable or malformed, so that a front end
    can show it.

    Provide the project root for a caller in the order the constraints give,
    starting from SUBZERO_PROJECT_DIR, or return the unresolved root error when no
    source gives one.
  }

  decisions {
    Skill folders resolve against the directory of the agent file and not the
    working directory, so the same file behaves the same from the server, the
    command line and the experiment harness.

    The host, the model and the thinking level live in model tiers and not in agent
    entries, so that what runs a child is set once per tier and an agent says only
    what it does. An agent that needs something else at one tier gets a
    tier override for it, as a docker-compose override replaces fields of a base
    service, and an agent that should run on another tier by default names it as
    its own tier. The default tier is required so that every agent entry resolves
    without a tier named.

    The general agent and the fallback agent are entries of the agents map that are
    named general and fallback, so that a name lookup, a tier and an explicit
    request find them like any agent and no agent name is reserved. They have no
    description, because the general agent is the default for work that names no
    agent and for tiered work, and neither is chosen by its description or offered
    to a parent for routing. The fallback agent takes the work of a name other than
    general that the agents map does not hold, and a front end's init writes it
    read-only so that a mistyped agent name cannot write to the repository or run
    shell commands, while tiered work keeps the tools of the general agent for
    compound-engineering reviewers. Only the host knows which tool names only read,
    so the load cannot enforce read-only, and a person who edits the entry owns
    that change.

    A model tier whose host is not supported still loads and only fails when it is
    used, so that one tier set for a host with no child driver does not stop every
    other delegation. Resolving at load and at each request gives tiered work the
    same model endpoint, parallel limit, timeout and frozen agent definition as a
    named agent. The routing settings live in the agent file so that the hooks and
    the server read one source, at the cost of being set per project and not per
    user.
  }
}
